"""Additional practical guidance for the Understandable lessons."""

PRACTICE = {
'3.1.1': """A multilingual application may replace its main content without loading a new document. When the predominant language changes, update the root language as part of the transition; when only a widget changes, mark that widget instead. Inspect the rendered document after navigation, not just the server template. A visitor's preferred language may differ from the article's actual language. Store the editorial language with each translation so a new locale does not require a developer to guess. Distinguish incorrect metadata from an unavailable speech voice: both affect the experience, but fixing one does not establish the other is available.""",
'3.1.2': """Consider a French quotation with an English passage nested inside it. Mark the quotation and the nested passage at their actual boundaries so inherited language follows the content. Avoid applying French to an enclosing section that also contains English instructions. Check the rendered output after an editor saves and publishes it: markup can be lost during conversion. Review the criterion's exceptions for proper names, technical terms, indeterminate language, and words incorporated into the surrounding language before treating them as failures. A speech test helps find errors, but the requirement concerns programmatic identification rather than guaranteeing a particular voice.""",
'3.1.3': """A glossary must define the sense used in the lesson, not merely list unrelated dictionary meanings. For example, clarify that a security challenge is a prompt used to verify identity when that is the intended sense. Place a short explanation where the word matters or link directly to an appropriate glossary entry. Verify the link target, keyboard access, and a clear way back to the passage. Ask a learner unfamiliar with the topic to explain the word in context. That exercise can reveal circular definitions which repeat the unfamiliar term without explaining it.""",
'3.1.4': """An abbreviation can have different expansions in different fields. Choose the expansion appropriate to the sentence and make it available to readers who enter directly at that page. An accessible glossary can support recurring terms, but its links should identify their targets and work without a mouse. If a supplementary definition appears in a popover, check that keyboard and touch users can open and dismiss it. Do not assume the title attribute on an abbreviation is reliably discoverable on every device. Check the stated exceptions before requiring expansions of familiar language forms.""",
'3.1.5': """A plain-language version must retain the conditions needed to complete the task. If an enrollment policy gives new and returning students different deadlines, name both groups and dates; saying only “apply early” loses the distinction. Break instructions into steps and explain unavoidable technical terms. Keep the alternative current when policy changes. Ask a representative reader to identify the correct deadline and documents rather than asking only whether the page looks simple. The lower-secondary threshold is a criterion for text, not a measure of a person's intelligence. A readability score alone does not establish understanding.""",
'3.1.6': """Attach pronunciation guidance to the occurrence where meaning would otherwise be ambiguous. If the same spelling has different meanings elsewhere, a generic link may leave the reader unsure which pronunciation applies. A written guide can complement audio for someone who cannot hear the recording. A notation familiar to specialists may be opaque to beginners, so explain it or offer a familiar equivalent. Check difficult examples with someone knowledgeable about the language and audience, particularly where pronunciation varies by region. The criterion addresses meaning dependent on pronunciation, not every unfamiliar word.""",
'3.2.1': """Test the actual workflow rather than searching for one event handler. Move to the control using Tab, move away with Shift+Tab, and repeat when a validation script returns focus to it. Record whether the URL, window, or focus position changes before activation. A focus ring or tooltip does not automatically constitute a context change. Replace a focus-triggered redirect with an explicit link or button, then test its keyboard activation. Confirm the accessible name describes the action or destination, so the user can decide whether to activate it.""",
'3.2.2': """A selector that reloads the page immediately can interrupt a keyboard user who is moving through choices. Put a clear warning before the control when a change of context occurs automatically, or provide an Apply button after the selection. Preserve other entered form values when appropriate. Test using the browser and assistive technologies supported by the product because selection behavior can differ. A small preview updated in place is not automatically a context change: observe whether the page, focus, or other substantial context actually changes before recording a failure.""",
'3.2.3': """Create an inventory of repeated navigation across several pages in the same set. Compare the relative sequence of common links rather than demanding identical screen coordinates or identical page-specific links. If Home, Courses, and Help appear in that order on one page but Help precedes Courses on another, the shared order differs. Open the mobile menu and examine keyboard order and visual presentation. Record a deliberately personalized order separately, since the criterion permits user-initiated changes. Comparing real page templates reveals discrepancies that a single screenshot cannot show.""",
'3.2.4': """Compare components by their actual function before comparing names. Searching the course catalog and searching support articles can need different labels because the scopes differ. Identical search functions in repeated headers should be identified consistently. Inspect the computed accessible name and visible text: an aria-label can override visible wording and produce inconsistent announcements. Record the page, visible label, accessible name, and resulting action. Fix the shared component where possible, then retest each template with keyboard and screen reader. Consistent identification concerns function, not identical decoration.""",
'3.2.5': """A tutorial that advances automatically can remove context while a learner is still reading. Offer an accessible way to turn off automatic advancement or let the learner choose Next. Make the setting discoverable before the transition and expose its current state. Trace scripted focus movement, new windows, and background navigation as well. Ordinary content updates do not all qualify as context changes, so record the result and how it affects the user's interaction. Test that the chosen setting remains effective when the learner returns to the workflow.""",
'3.2.6': """Map repeated help mechanisms across a set of pages, such as a contact link, self-help route, or automated contact option. Compare their order relative to other content rather than merely checking that an icon exists. A Help link after the navigation on one page but before it on another can disrupt a learned route. Inspect the mobile layout and source order as well as the desktop presentation. The criterion does not require every page to introduce a help service. It allows user-initiated changes in order when applicable.""",
'3.3.1': """After submitting an invalid form, review both the error summary and each affected field. A summary can link to errors while local messages identify each problem in text. Preserve entered values so a person can correct a mistake without starting over. Check server-reported errors as well as browser validation, since they may follow a different rendering path. Verify that messages remain discoverable when focus returns to fields and that a colored outline is not the sole cue. A generic “invalid” message may leave users guessing which item and problem need attention.""",
'3.3.2': """Base instructions on values the system actually accepts. If a date field accepts a specific format, provide it and an example before entry. Keep the visible label available after typing begins; placeholder text alone disappears. Associate the supporting description with the field and use a group label for related choices. Test at increased zoom and inspect the accessibility tree. Ask a new learner what information the field expects before submitting it. When requirements depend on an earlier choice, update the instructions accordingly and explain required indicators without relying only on color.""",
'3.3.3': """For a value outside a known permitted range, name the range when disclosure is safe and keep the entry available for correction. The advice must reflect the actual validation rule; stale help can cause repeated failure. For sign-in, a precise suggestion may reveal whether an account exists, so consider the security exception. Test messages after client-side and server-side rejection and ensure that the suggestion accompanies the detected error. Identifying an error and suggesting how to correct it are related but distinct tasks; a message should support both when the conditions apply.""",
'3.3.4': """Trace the transaction from entry to final commitment. For an applicable legal or financial submission, determine whether the safeguard is reversal, checking with a correction opportunity, or review and confirmation. A review screen must show meaningful details, allow edits, and submit corrected values rather than stale data. If reversal is the safeguard, verify that it can actually be performed and the outcome communicated. If checking is used, introduce a detectable error and confirm the user can correct it. Check the full scope of the criterion when selecting and evaluating the safeguard.""",
'3.3.5': """Help should answer a question relevant to the current task. A tax identifier field might need to explain where to find the number and show the accepted format. Longer help can be linked from the field to an accessible section or dialog; opening it should not erase entered values. Test opening, reading, closing, and returning with keyboard and screen reader. Keep the guidance aligned with validation rules. A generic support page can be useful, but check whether it actually provides the context-sensitive information needed for this field.""",
'3.3.6': """Apply the enhanced safeguard to the broader set of submissions covered by this criterion, including an ordinary profile update. Test the selected safeguard end to end. For reversal, change a value, undo it, and verify the original returns. For checking, introduce a detectable mistake and correct it. For review, confirm the summary includes the values, provides an edit route, and submits those edits. A warning with no usable action is not a working safeguard. Compare this broader coverage with the more limited situations in the related AA criterion.""",
'3.3.7': """Follow a single process and record the information already supplied. When the process needs it again, offer an editable prefilled value or a selection of the earlier value. For example, offer a shipping address for billing without forcing the two addresses to match. Verify that the chosen or edited value reaches final submission. Re-entry can be justified when it is essential, necessary for security, or earlier information is no longer valid; record the actual reason. This criterion concerns information needed again within the same process, not automatically every earlier visit.""",
'3.3.8': """Trace sign-in, required second factors, and recovery, identifying any cognitive function test along the route. Paste and password-manager support can assist with a password, but test that the form actually permits them. Where a step requires remembering or transcribing information, assess the available alternative or assistance and the criterion's stated exceptions. An option available only after sign-in cannot help someone blocked before sign-in. Verify that a learner can reach recovery, complete the necessary steps, and return to the protected task on supported devices.""",
'3.3.9': """Review every required authentication and recovery step against the enhanced criterion, not only the initial form. Object recognition and identifying personal content cannot rely on the exceptions available at the minimum level. If a cognitive function test remains, provide a qualifying alternative or assistance. A passkey helps only if the learner can enroll, use the available device, and recover access when the device is unavailable. Record each route and its dependencies, then test it with relevant assistive technology. Verify the alternative provides real authentication rather than an unprotected bypass.""",
}
GROUP_GUIDANCE = {
'3.1': """Review the published page and the learner's real task rather than an isolated sample. Record the relevant passage, its context, and how someone finds the explanation or language information. Inspect information exposed to software as well as the visible text. Test a representative route with keyboard and assistive technology where relevant. A successful test establishes what happened on that route; it does not prove every translation, speech voice, or later content update is correct. Recheck when editors change a passage, glossary entry, or translation, and record the result for the specific page and version reviewed.""",
'3.2': """Start at a realistic entry point and record the user's actions and resulting changes. Compare visible presentation with DOM order, keyboard route, accessible names, and any user preference relevant to the case. Test the default view and a narrow viewport when the same mechanism appears in both. Note whether the user initiated a change: an intentional selection and an unexpected transition create different experiences. Retest after modifying a shared component, since an older template may still exhibit the original problem. Record the specific pages, interaction steps, expected behavior, and actual behavior so another reviewer can reproduce the finding.""",
'3.3': """Follow the learner from the initial instructions through submission, recovery, and final confirmation. Try a realistic mistake, record the message and focus behavior, correct it, and verify the final data. Test the server response as well, because browser validation cannot cover every rejection. Use representative test values that do not expose real personal or financial information in the evidence. Review the experience using keyboard and, where relevant, a screen reader. Keep instructions, validation logic, and help aligned whenever a form or policy changes. Record the route tested and the resulting behavior so the team can reproduce the issue.""",
}
